Fluency Builder.

134
00:06:16,180 --> 00:06:22,900
All right, the first phrase we've got here is one that we often use in English, spoken English to talk about seeing our friends.

135
00:06:23,320 --> 00:06:26,400
Okay, so the first person says, do you want to hang out tomorrow?

136
00:06:26,760 --> 00:06:27,260
To hang out.

137
00:06:27,500 --> 00:06:27,860
That's right.

138
00:06:28,080 --> 00:06:28,960
So let's hang out.

139
00:06:29,200 --> 00:06:30,220
We never hang out anymore.

140
00:06:30,520 --> 00:06:31,040
What does that mean?

141
00:06:31,580 --> 00:06:36,520
Well, to hang out is it's hard to define because you can do many things when you're hanging out.

142
00:06:36,860 --> 00:06:39,300
But basically it means friends being together.

143
00:06:39,820 --> 00:06:46,070
So watching a movie, listening to music, chatting, these are all examples of hanging out.

144
00:06:46,410 --> 00:06:51,330
So maybe if you get together with your friend, just sit in the living room and talk, you're hanging out.

145
00:06:51,710 --> 00:06:51,990
That's right.

146
00:06:52,230 --> 00:06:55,310
Or you could go drive a car together and you're hanging out, you're talking.

147
00:06:55,580 --> 00:06:58,520
So to hang out is to be social, is to talk to your friends.

148
00:06:58,520 --> 00:06:59,470
To spend time together.

149
00:07:00,600 --> 00:07:03,360
And he said, wait, oh, I can't because I have a date.

150
00:07:03,450 --> 00:07:05,190
Okay, to have a date.

151
00:07:05,550 --> 00:07:10,910
So a date is a romantic appointment with someone that you want to go out with.

152
00:07:11,210 --> 00:07:12,570
But you have a date.

153
00:07:12,730 --> 00:07:13,510
This is something you have.

154
00:07:13,650 --> 00:07:14,690
I have a date that night.

155
00:07:15,070 --> 00:07:19,750
So this is actually a very important part because as you said, it's a romantic appointment.

156
00:07:20,030 --> 00:07:27,510
So if you have to go to the doctor and he's not your boyfriend or anything like this, you don't say I have a date with the doctor.

157
00:07:27,730 --> 00:07:30,080
No, you say I have an appointment with the doctor.

158
00:07:31,060 --> 00:07:36,680
But if it's romantic, you say, oh, I want to get my hair done because I have a date tonight.

159
00:07:36,680 --> 00:07:37,400
Right, I have a date.

160
00:07:37,700 --> 00:07:38,800
I want to look nice for my date.

161
00:07:39,200 --> 00:07:39,580
Okay.

162
00:07:40,080 --> 00:07:46,030
And then he goes on to explain that this date that he has is with a girl that he has been chatting with.

163
00:07:46,250 --> 00:07:48,890
Okay, so the verb here is to chat with, chat.

164
00:07:49,920 --> 00:07:57,310
Chat just means to talk to, but in internet terms, to chat with means to talk to on the internet.

165
00:07:58,160 --> 00:08:03,770
So maybe you use a program like MSN or Google Gchat to talk to somebody online.

166
00:08:04,120 --> 00:08:04,950
That's called chatting.

167
00:08:05,420 --> 00:08:05,960
That's right.

168
00:08:06,720 --> 00:08:15,160
So I think everyone has done this at one point or another when you are online chatting via MSN messenger or any of these chat clients.

169
00:08:15,200 --> 00:08:16,740
So very, very common thing.

170
00:08:16,960 --> 00:08:19,300
But remember to add the chat with.

171
00:08:19,600 --> 00:08:21,100
I am chatting with my friends.

172
00:08:21,560 --> 00:08:25,060
Yeah, I don't like to chat with people while I'm at work.

173
00:08:25,400 --> 00:08:25,660
Exactly.

174
00:08:26,560 --> 00:08:27,560
On the internet.

175
00:08:27,560 --> 00:08:34,040
So now this guy explained about the website match.com and he said that he signed up for this website.

176
00:08:34,040 --> 00:08:37,420
Okay, another really key verb when you're talking about internet things.

177
00:08:38,080 --> 00:08:43,920
So for example, if you want to come to EnglishPod, you can sign up for a free trial.

178
00:08:44,200 --> 00:08:46,040
That means provide your information.

179
00:08:46,040 --> 00:08:48,300
All right, start to use.

180
00:08:48,300 --> 00:08:48,420
That's right.

181
00:08:49,300 --> 00:08:52,080
So it applies for things online and offline.

182
00:08:52,080 --> 00:08:57,380
For example, in the real world or offline, you can sign up for a cooking class.

183
00:08:58,000 --> 00:08:58,120
That's right.

184
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:01,960
Or last week, I signed up for a dance class so I can learn how to dance.

185
00:09:02,560 --> 00:09:02,780
Very good.

186
00:09:03,560 --> 00:09:04,560
Okay.

187
00:09:04,560 --> 00:09:12,020
And then to finish off, the guy explained that he's tired of meeting people at bars or that his friends set up dates for him.

188
00:09:12,940 --> 00:09:15,540
Okay, so he doesn't like to be set up.

189
00:09:16,380 --> 00:09:19,460
To be set up, remember that B. To be set up.

190
00:09:19,600 --> 00:09:22,640
It means someone else is doing this to you.

191
00:09:22,640 --> 00:09:25,280
So Marco, I want to set you up with my friend.

192
00:09:25,560 --> 00:09:29,140
That means I want to take you and my friend and make you have a date.

193
00:09:29,480 --> 00:09:30,240
Right, exactly.

194
00:09:30,920 --> 00:09:32,300
So this is exactly what it means.

195
00:09:32,300 --> 00:09:38,360
To set someone up or to be set up is to have them go on a date with someone else.

196
00:09:38,700 --> 00:09:45,400
All right, so I don't like to be set up because I like to know the person I will have a date with before we go out.

197
00:09:45,880 --> 00:09:48,960
But when you're set up, someone else makes the decision for you.

198
00:09:49,280 --> 00:09:50,280
That's right.

199
00:09:50,280 --> 00:09:51,280
It's a little bit difficult.

200
00:09:51,280 --> 00:09:52,280
That's right.

201
